# These three extfs helpers exist to show what dpkg knows: apt+ lists the
# packages of the system, dpkg+ opens one of them as a directory, and debd shows
# the files a package installed. There is no dpkg command that gives a listing
# in the form a panel reads, so they read /var/lib/dpkg themselves.
mcommander: uses-dpkg-database-directly [usr/lib/mcommander/extfs.d/apt+]
mcommander: uses-dpkg-database-directly [usr/lib/mcommander/extfs.d/debd]
mcommander: uses-dpkg-database-directly [usr/lib/mcommander/extfs.d/dpkg+]

# The syntax files are not UTF-8 on purpose. Each of these has one line that
# lists the bytes from 0x20 to 0xFF as a character class, which is how the
# editor highlights any printable character inside a literal. The engine
# matches bytes and not characters: src/syntax/syntax.c fills a set of 256
# entries, one per byte. Recoded to UTF-8 the class would cover 0xC2, 0xC3 and
# 0x80 to 0xBF instead, so it would lose 0xC4 to 0xFF, and Cyrillic, Greek and
# everything else outside Latin-1 would stop matching.
mcommander: national-encoding [usr/share/mcommander/syntax/*.syntax]
